People of Macedonia voted in order to change the name of the state
The Makedonia people voted on Sunday to secregate the country's nasy to the "Republic of North Macedonia," an action that will resolve a decade-long dispute with Greece that has banned its offer of membership to become part of the European Union and NATO.
Greeze Greece, which has a province called Macdonia, maintains an opinion that the name of its northern neighbor represents an acknowletion of ownership of its territory and has banned the joining of the country with NATO and the European Union.
The two sides of the government reached an agreement in June based on a new name put forward, but the nationalist opposition argued that the change would damage the identity of a large-dollar population of Slavia Makedonia.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he wouldn't cast his ballot at the general election and a boycott campaign had indignant instilled any hesitating whether the results of the election could reach a minimum of 50 per cent of the votes needed in order for election results to be declared valid.
The question contained in the ballot read: "Whether do you agree to the membership of NATO and the EU by accepting an agreement with Greece."
Proponder of name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argues that it is a reasonable price to be paid in order to obtain entry permits into bodies such as the European Union and NATO for Macedon, one of the countries born from the destruction of Yugoslavia.
"I've come today to vote for the future of the country, for the sake of the younger generation of Macedonia so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union because it means a safer life for all of us," says 79-year-old Olivera Georgijevska in Skopje.
Although it is not legally binding, quite a number of lawmakers say they will comply with the results of the ballot to make a decision.
The name change requires two-third-per-for-three votes in parliament.
The state elector's Commission says there have been no reports of violations until 1 p.m.
However, it turns out that the voting results only reached 16 per cent, compared with 34 per cent in last parliamentary elections in 2016 when 66 per cent of registered voters provided their ballots.
"I've come to give my ballot for the sake of my children, our place is in Europe," Gjose Tanevski, a 62-year-ear-year-old Gjose Tanevki, a voter in the capital, Skopje.
Macdonia Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and her son Dushko gave their ballots for general elections in Macdonia in order to rename the country that will pave the way for the country to merge with NATO and the European Union in Strumica, Macedonia, September 30, 2018.
In front of parliament in Skopje, the 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov, had already prepared a small stage and pulled the seats in front of the tents created by those who would boycott the ballot.
"We agreed to join NATO and the European Union, but we would like to join honorably, not through the door of waitresses," Kavadarkov said.
"We are a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don't want to accept us as Macedonia, we can move on to China and Russia as well as being part of Euro-Asia integration."
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO's membership will bring in more necessary investment for Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of more than 20 per cent.
"I'm sure most of the population will approve it because more than 80 per cent of our society supports the European Union and NATO," Zaev said after delivering his ballot.
He said the "yes" outcome will be "confirmation of our future."
An unveinion poll published by the Institute for the Research Institute of the Makedonia Policies on Monday stated that between 30 and 43 per cent of voters will take part in the ballot - less than the amount needed.
Another poll, organized by Telma Makedonia's TV station, found that there were 57 per cent of responders planning to co-wrain their vote on Sunday.
Of them, 70 per cent said they would vote yes.
In order for the vote to be successful, it takes 50 per cent plus 1 vote.
A failure in the ballot will represent a first-device rejection of the pro-western government since taking over in May last year.
