The Labor Party is no longer a place of public service.
Now be intolerant to anyone who raises his mind
When the Momentum Activist in the local party I gave a vote to denounce me, it was not surprising.
After all, I'm in the last order of Members of Parliament, who are told that our presence is not expected - it's all because we've expressed an opinion.
My collion in the parliament, Joan Ryan got similar treatment because he defended antisemitism decisively.
In my case, the deviation movement criticized me for disagreeing with Jeremy Corbyn.
Regarding the importance of responsible economic policies, on national security, in Europe, issues that are ironically the same as those opposed by Jeremy Corbyn with previous leaders.
A notification for the meeting of eastern Nottingham workers on Friday stated that "we want the meeting to be inclusive and proliquential."
During my nearly eight-year-old I became a member of the local Labor Parliament, the meeting of the General Committee on Friday Night was exactly like that.
Unfortunately on this day, the nature of many meetings and appointments regarding politics that are "beneaous, friendlier" instead of having been forgotten for a long time, if indeed it had once begun.
Disagreements in the labor party are increasingly obsolete untolerated and every opinion is judged by the authority of the party.
It began as soon as Jeremy became a leader, as a colleague I had thought I would be able to share the same political views, starting to expect me to go back-to-forth and accept a position that I would never have been willing to accept - both in national security and the EU's single market.
Whenever I speak openly - and no matter what I say - then after that there will be a slack on social media asking for an abortion of appointments, reproaching central politics, telling me I shouldn't have served in the Labour party.
And it's not just me who experienced it.
Absolutely, I know that I'm luckier compared to some of my colleagues because the comments addressed to me tend to be political.
I am admiring the professionalism and the limitations of my colleagues facing sexual harassment or racism every day but have never dodged.
One of the most disapproving aspects in today's political era is how the level of abuse has been considered reasonable.
Jeremy Corbyn claimed last week that the Labour party was supposed to help develop a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer in a wider church and with every motion of “insecurity” or changing the selection of party rules to be more limited.
I get a lot of advice over the past two years that prompted me to still bow my head, didn't voice an opinion, and then I'm going to say "good."
But I jumped into politics not to do that.
Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago when I was still a school-studnaughter, provoked by Thatcher's waiver that resulted in my comprehensive school classes literally collapsing, I've been trying to fight for a better public service for those who desperately need it - both as local counselors and ministers in the government.
I've never concealed my political action, including at the last election.
No one in East Nottingham will question my policy positions and things I don’t agree on in the current leadership.
For those advocating a motion on Friday, all I can say is when the country is moving rapidly towards a division of sovereignty (Brexit), it will jeopardize our country's businesses and public services, I don't want a waste of time and energy for allegiance to Labour party leaders.
But really, one of my messages is not for Nottingham Momentum, it is for my constituent, as a member of the Labour party or not. I am proud to serve you and I promise that there is no threat of revocation or a political policy that will prevent me from doing what I believe is the best interest for all of you.
Chris Leslie is a member of parliament (MP) for the East of Nottingham
