John Edward: Lingual expertise is very important for global citizens
Scotland's private school maintains a track record of academic excellence, and this continues in 2018 with outstanding exam results, which are further strengthened by every student and collective success in sports, art, music as well as hard work.
With more than 30,000 students throughout Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Private School Board (SCIS) went to great lengths to provide the best service for the students and the elderly.
Self-employed schools aim to prepare their students for advanced and higher education, the careers of their choice, and their role as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector that can design and implement school curricula as expected, we see the modern language continues to be a popular and desirable elective subject in many schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you're talking to someone in a language he's understood, your words will go into his head.
If you are talking to him in his language then your speech will come into his heart.”
It's a strong reminder that we can't only rely on English if we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From the results of this latest exam this year, we can see that the language is the top position of the league table with the highest graduation scores in self-scholarship.
A total of 68 per cent of students who are studying a foreign language get an A Plus score.
The data, collected from 74 SCIS member schools showing that 72 per cent of students get A Plus scores in Mandarin, while 72 per cent of those who learned German, 69 per cent of those who learned French and 63 per cent who learned Spanish also got an A grade.
This shows that self-sufficiency schools in Scotland support foreign languages as an important skill that children and adolescence in the foreseeable future.
Today the languages, as the choice of the subjects, are held in the same standard as STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the modern school curriculum and in other places.
A survey conducted conducted by the British Employment and Skills Service in 2014 found that of some of the reasons why Employers had to continue to struggle to fill vacancies, 17 per cent why it was a lack of language skills.
Therefore, lactation skills are increasingly recommended to prepare teenagers for the sake of their careers in the future.
With the increasing number of job opportunities that require language skills, this skill is important in the global world.
No matter the choice of one’s career, if they have already learned a second language, in the future they will feel the real benefits of having this kind of lifelong skills.
Being able to communicate directly with the residents of a foreign country will automatically make a multi-lingual person excel in the competition.
According to a YouGov poll on more than 4,000 English adults in 2013, 75 per cent of them didn't speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation and French became the only language spoken in double-digit percentage, which was 15 per cent.
Here's why investing in language teaching for today's children is important at the moment.
Having multi-lingual skills, especially the languages of countries with a developing economy, will equip children with a better opportunity to find a meaningful job.
In Scotland, every school will teach different languages.
Some of the schools will focus on more classical modern languages, while others will teach languages that are considered to be most important in the UK for the next few years, i.e. 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
No Matter your child's interest, there will always be a number of languages to choose from in a standalone school, with teaching staff who are experts in this field.
Scotland's self-sustaining school is intended to provide a learning environment that will prepare the children and equip them with the skills needed to succeed, whatever happens in the future.
Undeniably that at the present time, in a global business environment, language will always be an important aspect in determining the future of the country, so this must be reflected in education.
In fact, the modern language should really be regarded as "international communication skills."
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity, and excellence for Scottish teenagers.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is the Director of Scotland's Standing-Scholarship Board
