Harvard learners take courses on adequate rest
A new course at Harvard University this year makes all undergraduate students there sleep even more, in an effort to fight macho culture in developing learning through 'human owl-powered' caffeine-powered.
An academia found that students at the world's number one university often don't know anything if it's discussing very basic things about how to take care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, professor of medicine specializing in sleep problems at Harvard Medical School and specialists in Brigham and Women's Hospital, designed a course, which she believes was the first of her type in the U.S.
He was inspired to start the course after providing lectures regarding the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
"In the end a girl came to me and said: 'whether I've only been told now, in my final year?'
The girl said no one ever told about the importance of sleeping - which surprised me," she told The Telegraph.
The courses that were first-located this year, explain to the students about the essence of how good sleep habits help academic and athletic outcomes, as well as improving their general well-being.
Paul Barreira, psychiatric professor at the Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health services, said that the university decided to introduce this course after finding highly sleep deprived students throughout the week.
This one-on-hour course involves a series of interactive tasks.
In one session there is an overview of the dormitory rooms, in which students click on coffee cups, curtains, sweatpants, and books to get an explanation of the effects of caffeine and lights, as well as how athletic results are influenced by sleep deprivation, and the importance of bedtime routines.
In another part, participants got an explanation of how prolonged sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression, and cancer.
A campus map, with an interactive icon, then encourages attendees to think about their daily routine.
'We knew this wouldn't change student behaviour instantly.
But we're confident that they've got the right to know - just as you've got the right to know the effect of choosing to sipping cigarettes on health," Czeisler added.
The proud culture of 'stay' is still there, he said, plus that modern technology and the ever-increasing pressure on students which means sleep deprivation is an ever-developing problem.
Ensuring you get enough to sleep, with good quality, it has to be a 'secret weapon' to fight stress, fatigue, and anxiety, he said - even to avoid weight gain, as sleep deprivation makes the brain activate the hunger mode, causing constant hunger.
The 19-year-old caloric resident, a 19-year-old califian who studied chemistry and physical biology who helped Professor Czeisler designed the course, has taken one of his classes last year during his first year at Harvard.
He said that this course had opened his eyes and inspired him to organize the course for the entire campus.
The next step, he hopes that all postgraduate students will be instructed to complete a similar study program before joining a competitive institution.
Czeisler recommends that college students should be considering setting a sleep alarm, as well as a wake-wake alarm, and be aware of the effects of 'blue luminescence' emitted by electronic screens and LED lights, which can shift your circadian's rhythms out of its set lanes thereby causing sleep problems.
