Almanac: Geiger's Inventor
And now from the Almanac "Sunday Morning" page: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago on this day, and STILL WILL CONCERN CORRRENCILUDE ... the day when future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, it is an invention that ultimately makes the device referred to as Geiger Vocabulary.
Becoming the mainstay of science since then, Geiger Shrewrs has also become a mainstay of pop culture, as in the 1950 film "Bells of Coronado," starring cowboy scientists who looked less convincing namely Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
A man: What kind of thing is that?”
Rogers: "It's Geiger Shredder, it was used to locate the location of radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
When using these earphones, you’ll actually be able to hear an atomic effect released by radioactivity in minerals.”
Evans: "Say, it's definitely popping up now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the discovery that made his name remain alive.
New cancer vaccinants teach the immune system to 'know' nasty cells
New cancer vaccinates teach immune systems to 'recognize' nasty cells and kill them
Vaccines teach the immune system to recognize bad cells as part of treatment
The method involves the process of extracting immune cells from the patient, changing it in the laboratory
They can then 'recognize' proteins that are common in many cancers and then be injected again
An experimental vaccine shows promising results in patients with a wide variety of cancers.
A woman is treated with such a vaccine that teaches the immune system to recognize nasty cells, her ovarian cancer disappears for more than 18 months.
This method involves the process of extracting the patient's immune cells, changing it in the laboratory so that they can "regulate" proteins that are commonly found in many cancers called HER2, and then re-inautify those cells.
Professor Jay Berzofsky, from the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda in Bethesda, said: "The results show that we have a very promising vaccine."
HER2 "encourages the growth of several types of cancers," including breast, ovary, pulmonary and colorectal cancer, Prof Berzofsky explained.
Equal approaches such as the retrieval of a patient's immune cells and "teaching" to their way of proficiency targeting cancer cells have been successful in treating one type of leukemia.
