HIV/Aids: China reports 14% surge in new cases
China has announced a 14% increase in its population living with HIV and Aids.
More than 820,000 people are contracted in this country, health workers said.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018.
The majority of new cases are transmitted through sex, marking changes from before.
Traditionally, HIV spreads rapidly through several parts of China as a result of an infected blood transfusion.
However, the number of people suffering from HIV in this way has dreastered to almost zero, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, year-to-Year-of-a-Year numbers living with HIV and Aids in China have increased by up to 100,000.
Transmission of HIV through sex is an acute problem in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discriminate against LGBT people is increasingly rife.
Due to the conservative value of this country, the study estimates that 70-90% of men who have sexual relations with men will eventually marry a woman.
Plentiful transmission of the disease comes from inadequate sexual protection in this relationship.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has promised a universal access for the treatment of HIV as part of an attempt to deal with the problem.
