What is a Global Citizen Festival Means and Has this event Already Becoming Done Something to Reduce Poverty?
On this Saturday, the New York will hold a Global Citizen Festival, an annual music event featuring a very impressive array of stars, and an memorable mission; ending the world's poverty.
Now, in the seventh year, the Global Citizen Festival will bear witness tens of thousands of people to come flocking to the Great Lawn in Central Park not just to enjoy action from Janet Jackson, Cardi B, and Shawn Mendes.
The Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is a continuation of the Global Poverty Project, an international advocacy group that hopes to end poverty by escalating the number of people actively battling poverty.
In order to get free tickets for the event (unless you want to pay for VIP tickets), concertgoers must complete a series of assignments or “actions” such as volunteering, sending emails to world leaders, calling, or other meaningful ways to help raise awareness of their goal of ending poverty.
However, how successful does Global Citizen for the past 12 years in achieving its goals?
Is the idea of awarding a gift in the form of a free concert is the right way to invite people to do an action, or just another case called "clicktivism" - people feel it looks like they're making a real difference by signing an online petition or submitting a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen says it has recorded more than 19 million "actions" from its prophesies, pushing to set other goals.
It is said that this action has helped spur the world's leaders to announce commitments and policies equivalent to more than $37 billion that will have an effect on more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group has garnered 390 commitments and announcements derived from their actions, at least $10 billion of whom have been channeled or fundraising.
This group estimates that safe funds so far have had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Several key commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a UK-based partnership consisting of investors and executors committed to "helping children grow to their maximum potential," pleasting $35 million to Rwanda to help end malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizen.
"With support from the UK's governments, contributors, national governments, and Global Citizens like you, we can make social inequities regarding malnutrition a footnote in history," The Power of Nutrition ambassador Tracey Ullman told the crowd during a live concert in London in April 2018.
This group also said that after more than 5,000 actions were carried out to improve nutrition for mothers-and-daughters in the UK, the government announced funds for a project, the Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children through nutrition.
Responding to one of the questions in the General Q&A on their website asking "what makes you think we can put an end to this extreme poverty?"
Global citizens replied: "It's going to be a long and difficult path - sometimes we're going to fall and fail.
However, just like the great civic and anti-apartheid rights movements that have existed before us, we will succeed, because if we are united we will get stronger.
Janet Jackson, Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are some of the stars who appeared on this year's show in New York, who will be hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
