National Trust listens to bat secret lives discreetly
The new research was carried out on an estate in the Scottish Height Plateau aimed at uncovering the way the bats used landscapes in their food hunt.
It's expected that these findings will provide a new light on the behavior of these unique flying mammals and help guide conservation activities in the foreseeable.
The study conducted by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will follow the common cheekstrollelles as well as long-eared brown bats and Daubenton at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special cuppers will be placed in the main location around the property to track bat activity throughout the season.
NHS staff and the volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using handheld detectors.
Voting experts analysing all the recordings will ascertain the frequency of bat calls and which species carry out what activity.
Then maps and habitat reports will be created to create an image with a detailed landscape scale about their behavior.
Rob Dewar, a nature conservation adviser for NTS, hopes these results will reveal which habitat areas are most important for bats and how each species uses.
This information will help determine what benefits are gained from habitat-management works such as the manufacture of pastures and the best ways to raise forests for bats and other related species.
The population of the bats in Scotland and throughout the UK declined sharply in the last few centuries.
This bat is threatened with the development and development work that affects the nest and the loss of the habitat.
Wind turbines and illumination also pose a risk, such as sticky paper hung to snare the bat as well as the chemical treatment of building materials, as well as attacks from pet cats.
In fact the bat is not blind.
However, due to his habit of hunting at night, his ears are more useful than his eyes in terms of catching prey.
They use sophisticated echo location techniques to find insects appropriately and a barrier in their flying lanes.
NTS, which is responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens, and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, is very concerned for bats.
Have ten trained experts, who routinely conduct surveys, nest inspections, and sometimes rescue efforts.
The organization has also set up Scotland's first and only nature reserves on Threave plantations in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight out of ten bat species in Scotland.
Healing manger David Thompson said the plantation is a suitable area for bats.
"Here in Threave, we've got a good area for bats," he said.
"We've got old-fashioned buildings, a lot of old-fashioned trees, and it's a good habitat.
However, there's still a lot we don't already know about bats, so, the work we're doing here and in other properties will help us better understand about what they need to evolve."
He emphasized the importance of a bat's examination before carrying out maintenance inside the property, as unwittingly there is the possibility of the destruction of one maternity nest can kill up to 400 female and still small bats, which will probably wipe out the entire local population.
The bat is protected and killing, harassing, or interfering, or shattering, or shattering its nest is an illegal act.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish official for the Trust's Coservation, has encouraged the general public to help.
He said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats and for many of our species that we don't yet know about the fate of their populations."
