Below are some of the main facts about the city of Palu and Donggala, on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the provincial capital of Central Sulawesi province, located at the narrow end of the bay on the west coast of the island of Sulawesi, with an approximate population of 379,800 in 2017.
When earthquakes and tsunamis hit, the city was celebrating its 40th Anniversary.
Donggala, meanwhile, is an area of more than 300 km ($1300 miles) from the coastline in the north-west island of Sulawesi.
This district, which is an administrative area under the province, is estimated to have 299,200 inhabitants in 2017.
The mainstay economic eye in the province of South Sulawesi is to look for fish and grow crops, especially on Donggala coast.
However, the existence of nickel mining also plays an important role in the province, even though it is mostly centered in Morowali, across the coast of Sulawesi.
Recorded in the last 100 years, Palu and Donggala have been hit a tsunami several times, based on data from the Indonesian Disaster Management Agency.
In 1938, more than 200 people died in a tsunami and hundreds of homes in Donggala were devastated.
In 1996, nine people died in a tsunami in western Donggala.
Seismically, Indonesia is located in the Pacific Ring of Fire and therefore earthquakes will occur on a regular basis.
A number of major earthquakes and tsunamis that have struck in recent years are as follows:
In 2004: December 26, Indonesia was hit by a major earthquake, precisely on the west coast of the province of Aceh, in northern Sumatra, which also triggered a tsunami in 14 countries, resulting in 226,000 lives along the Indian Ocean coastline, and more than half of the death toll was in Aceh.
From 2005: The end of March and the beginning of April, a series of strong earthquakes have hit the west coast of Sumatra.
On Nias Island, off the coast of Sumatra, hundreds of people were killed.
In 2006: The 6.8-magnitude earthquake hit the south of Java, Indonesia's most pop-intensive island, triggering a tsunami on the south coast and resulted in nearly 700 people dying.
Year 2009: There was an earthquake with a strength of 7.6 near the city of Padang, the provincial capital of West Sumatra.
The death toll amounted to more than 1,100.
In 2010: One of the islands in the Mentawai archipelago, apart from Sumatra, was also hit by a 7.5-magnitude earthquake that triggered a 10-meter-tall tsunami that instantly destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
In 2016: Pidie Jaya Regency, Aceh, also experienced a shallow earthquake and resulted in destruction, as well as the panic of people who were reminded of the deterioration due to deadly earthquakes and tsunamis in 2004 ago.
Although this time there is not a potential tsunami, more than 100 people died due to the collapse of the building.
In the year of 2018: Lombok, dubbed as a tourist island, also experienced a large earthquake that killed more than 500 people, most of which on the north side of the island.
The euncreation destroyed thousands of buildings and temporarily ravaged thousands of tourists.
