Pyongyang made no public comment.
Most deflectors from the North are eventually offered South Korean citizenship.
Seoul says more than 30,000 people from North Korea have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most escaped through China, which has the longest border with North Korea and is easier to cross than the Demilitarized Zone between the two Koreas.
China treats defectors as illegal migrants rather than disposers and often force repatriates them.
Relations between the North and the South - who are technically still in the war - have greatly improved in the last few months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries were met in Pyongyang for talks centered on pending denuclearization negotiations.
This comes after a historic June meeting between U.S. president Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, when they agreed in the broadest sense to co-operate about a nuclear-free Korean peninsula.
